Z||000000||GT Bastard||08-16-2000||03:00 AM||||I've been trying different methods of coping with stress at work.  At first I hid behind my sense of humor and sarcasm, but that wasn't working.  Then I had an idea...  I figured I would just visualize myself in a happy place at pretend that my body is being controlled remotely(I have a knack for this.)  This way things don't affect me as directly.  It's almost like watching life go by in past-tense.  It cuts down on my stress level, but I've realized there are flaws in this behavior.  After a reletively short period of time, I begin to lose emotion and become somewhat zombie-like.  Some people seem to believe that even if you spend most of your day unhappy, a single joyous moment can totally make up for it, and we should live for those moments and not the negative ones.  I'm beginning to take interest in this train of thought.  What do you think?  Ultimately I'd like to let the negativity in the world to be filtered out, leaving only what is good and true.  Can a person train themselves to do this?  I think maybe....  <P>That's enough for now.  It's a full moon tonight. ||||reg||3
Z||000001||Taijiguy||08-16-2000||11:37 AM||||At the risk of sounding COMPLETELY off my rocker, I can impart a bit of insight I have been given over the years.<P>ALL stress is the result of the mind resisting life. When you realize that things that cause stress in you, originate in you, you can choose differently. It's recognizing the cause, and accepting responsibility for it. Nothing you experience can truly be traced to anything OUTSIDE of you.<P>Also, when you realize that ALL negative feelings are the result of fear (or ego) you can begin to understand their origin, and deal with them at the source, not the symptom. It's like the check engine light coming on. You can say there's something wrong with the light (equal to the stress you feel) or you can recognize the light is on because you have a bad map sensor-which is the true cause (that would be equal to the fear or ego that's causing the stress)<P>It's hard to swallow, and goes against everything you're taught, but when you accept responsibility for how you feel, you can begin to make changes. I have actually reached a place where MOST of the time, I feel like I'm on vacation. Everything I do is about being on vacation. It takes conscious effort, but MAN, does it make life FUN!<P>-Taiji (aka Buddhadude)  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000002||fropuff||08-16-2000||01:18 PM||||I agree.  ||||reg||1
Z||000003||1FST2M6||08-16-2000||01:27 PM||||i can go for that too.. i like the single joyous moment too... crappy week at work. 13 of the fastest seconds down the track.. I don't ever remember what happend all week, not sure if i don't remember or just don't care cause all the adrenelin.. just make sure your girlfriend isn't forgotten too!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> she doesn't like that...   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by 1FST2M6 (edited 08-16-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||Cliff Pennock||08-16-2000||01:45 PM||||I agree with Taijiguy. I've learned how to cope with stress many years ago by realising that stress indeed originates from within yourself. It's buried deep inside our DNA and it used to be a protection mechanism when we were still somewhere on the bottom of the food-chain. Stress only serves as a signal to your brain to generate some hormones (corticosteroids and catecholamins) that will keep you paranoid. Remember, paranoia is the best way to survive when you are chased by a predator!<P>It can be supressed however. I have no idea how that works but speaking for myself, the realisation that stress does not help you solve the problems you're stressed about, will on itself lower your stress-level. By now I'm at the point that hardly anything stresses me out and I'm much more capable of solving problems than I used to be.<P>I have learned that this approach works for a lot of things. For instance, I have a very high pain threshold (is that the proper way to say it in english?). The realisation that pain is nothing more than your body's way of signalling the brain something is wrong (and to get your immune system going), helps to supress the pain. Now pain doesn't bother me that much anymore. If I bump my knee for instance, I just tell myself "Alright already! I know I just bumped my knee so no sense in keep telling me that!". I know it might sound rediculous but for me it really works.||||reg||1
Z||000005||DJRice||08-17-2000||09:14 AM||||I can relate a bit as well.  Right now, my project here at work is suffering financial problems (who isnt) and it has driven the stress level up for all members of my team.  Sometimes it seems like we are set up to fail, and it use to seriously frustrate me.  <P>When I stepped back and realized that the crappy mood at work was bleeding over into my personal life and time, I decided to find a way to better deal with work.  In a nutshell, I re-evaluated my "sphere of influence".  I do the best job I can, and I recognize that there are some things that are beyond my influence.  If I cant change it, why let it work me up.  I am very aware of what I can control now, and I let the rest ride. <P>My dad use to have a small typed sign on his desk.  I would look at it as a kid and wonder what in the world it meant.  Now I understand.  <P>"I will not let someone else decide how I am going to act (or feel)."<P>Hope this helps!<P>------------------<BR>Dillon<BR>Black '87 SE V6<BR>"You mean like the back seat of a Volkswagen?"||||reg||1
Z||000006||Taijiguy||08-18-2000||08:08 AM||||Here's a little smattering from one of my favortie compositions, that goes with that:<P>"Go placidly amid the noise and the haste, and remember what peace there may be in silence..."<P>-Taiji||||reg||1
Z||000007||Fret Naught||08-18-2000||12:33 PM||||STRESS!!!!! ME?<BR>I am not a drug addict or alcholoic nor am I involved in a 12 step program, however, I often ponder and recite their creed the serinity prayer. God grant me the serinity to accept the things I can not change, the courage to change the things I can and the wisdom to know the difference.  Being a type A personallity, I have had to learn to deal with stess, for me it boiled down to lack of control. I would stess when I was not in control. Like Taij. said the root cause was much deeper and ultimately I had to face and take responsibility for the baggage, but the start is realizing that you have no control over any one or anything except yourself.<BR>Just my two cents worth.<BR>Happiness, joy, peace, contentment, serinity, all come from within and not from the external if you allow yourself to be controlled by the external, then you are but a rutterless ship tossed to and fro by wind and wave.||||reg||7
